ust was um a burning  desire and you know i worked um through  media through politics started out at  fox news as a producer  left got involved with trump world and  the rnc and the trump campaign and then  the trump white house and it just was  kind of a step-by-step ladder  progression and one that i believe  and the title you see for such a time as  this that god wanted me there in that  moment for such a time as this um in  that time during covet 19 as press  secretary so you were a conservative  basically from eight years old and on  oh yes born and raised a conservative it  always made a lot more sense to me than  the other side  i've come to agree with you um all right  so let's talk a little bit about the the  press secretary portion of this because  i find the press secretary  the job itself to me is like one of the  most fascinating jobs on earth that in  essence you have an administration that  has talking points you have reporters  that are supposed to be asking you  contrarian questions but in your case it  was like  it wasn't just contrarian questions it  was trying to basically destroy you guys  every day  what were what were some of your kind of  guiding principles as you would you know  go into that room every day  so when i first thought about taking the  job you know i came in and i thought i'm  going to give the press a chance i know  who i'm up against this is the liberal  media i know they're going to be unfair  i've watched what they've done to trump  for three plus years um but i said let's  give it a chance and i said i'll have a  red line should they cross that red line  of going personal with me um i'll be  prepared to fight back do so politely  and gently and gracefully but i will  fight back and i won't be pummeled and  run over so my dad said to me come up  with a motto for your office and i said  offense only that's our motto we're  going to be on offense we're not going  to be the republican that lays down and  takes it we're not going to be the  administration that uh you know loses an  argument we're going to be the  administration that brings the truth to  the american people but brings what i  think is uh what is needed most with  this press corps accountability with a  capital a uh we'll read their headlines  to them because i believe in  accountability so we'll fight back  offense only that was our motto it's the  whole title of a chapter in my book so  you did just that i mean you would read  headlines back to them you would call  them out on stories that they butchered  or ignored you'd show video clips i mean  nobody really i don't think did that  before you and saki certainly doesn't do  it since show video clips exposing the  nonsense that they were putting forth  did you feel like you were getting  through to them or that they just i mean  as most of my audience i think believes  at this point they just have their  agenda they were there to destroy you  and they're basically there to now  protect the administration  yeah i think i did get through to them i  think i remember as my second briefing  it was the first time  that i kind of read headlines back to  them because i told you that red line  was crossed  jeff mason asked a very personal  question um a out of context video clip  that um based on a remark that i made  before covid hit back when fauci was out  there saying oh cloven has a meninsual  threat to our country i was echoing that  same line and uh they took that and  asked me about it in briefing number two  and you know i had conveniently just  read all of their misinformation on  covet literally right before going to  the podium so i had that tabbed and  ready to go and uh jeff mason who's  actually a nice person so i feel bad  that he was the one that the headline uh  reading happened to um but you know i  could see the audible shock on their  faces um because i was told coming into  the job half of your job is to work for  the press half of it's to work for the  president but when the press has stopped  working for the people who i'm  ultimately working for um my job's not  have to work for the press anymore my  job's to work for the american people  and to me that meant reading the  headlines i do think it broke through to  them at least a little  what was the stress like just like  personally i mean it's not the pro it's  probably not the most fun job on earth  i'm sure you get some kicks out of it  and there's some fun moments but like  you know you were basically enemy number  one for these people because you were  you know sort of guarding trump in  essence  yeah i mean there were definitely  stressful days like one that comes to  mind is uh i was literally about to go  to the podium i think it was one o'clock  and the woodward book dropped that the  audio recordings on cnn so you can  imagine i prepared a whole briefing on  whatever else the news of the day was  and  moments before my briefing it's almost  as if cnn had planned it uh the drop and  so very little time to prepare i didn't  have time to call people across the  administration just had to do the best i  could so days like that are stressful  but i will say we had a lot of fun and i  think that's why i believe my press shop  excelled is because we had fun with the  press we love brainstorming all the  ridiculous things they could ask us and  oftentimes we would find the absolute  hardest question to answer we'd identify  it but because most of them don't do  their homework usually the hardest  question was never asked so i've heard  you answered this question before but  can you talk a little bit about what  trump's relationship with you was like  because you were basically the filter to  the press if he wasn't just jumping on  twitter  yeah so he was very  appreciative of me i think he loved the  way i held the press accountable and  liked my style um so i mean i remember  reading about his relationship with  members of the administration before i  came in and w